4.0 Dispersal Mechanism - CAD Animation Sequence
4.1 Time Sequence for Animation:
T = 0.000s (Detonation)

Central PETN charge detonates (model as quick flash, not fireball)

Shockwave propagates radially at ~8000 m/s

T = 0.001s (Membrane Rupture)

6 alginate membranes rupture simultaneously

Liquid compartments pressurized to 30 psi begin expanding

T = 0.005s (Powder Fluidization)

Dry powder matrix aerated by expanding gases

Liquid and powder begin turbulent mixing

Shell experiences 50-100 psi internal pressure

T = 0.010s (Shell Fracture)

Shell fails along hexagonal dimple patterns

Fragments into ~1000 small pieces (2-5cm each)

Biodegradable pieces begin dispersion

T = 0.020s (Gel Formation & Expansion)

Mixed components form aerated gel

Expansion cloud reaches 2m diameter sphere

Initial velocity: 50-100 m/s radially outward

T = 0.100s (Maximum Expansion)

Gel cloud diameter: 8-10 meters

Velocity reduced to 20-30 m/s

Cloud begins descending toward ground

T = 1.000s (Ground Impact & Coating)

Gel impacts vegetation/ground

Forms 1.5-3mm thick coating

Adhesion to vertical and horizontal surfaces

4.2 Key Physics to Visualize:
Shockwave propagation (transparent pressure wave)

Turbulent mixing (swirling liquid/powder interaction)

Fracture pattern (shell breaking along engineered weak points)

Gel "stretching" (showing cohesive, stringy properties)

Surface adhesion (gel sticking to pine needles, bark, ground)
